# Step 08 — Cluster access (admin user + oc login)
# Account: 759945100724  OCM: stage  Date: 2026-10-12
#
# The generated cluster-admin password has been redacted. `rosa create admin`
# prints it once to the terminal and it is not recoverable afterwards -- if you
# lose it, delete and recreate the admin user. Never commit it.

## Why this step is needed at all
#
# ROSA does NOT behave like a self-managed `openshift-install` cluster:
#   - there is no auth/kubeconfig written anywhere
#   - there is no `kubeadmin` user and no kubeadmin-password file
#
# A freshly created HCP cluster has NO way to authenticate. Confirmed:
#   $ AWS_PROFILE=saml rosa list idps  -c alebedev-test
#   INFO: There are no identity providers configured for cluster 'alebedev-test'
#   $ AWS_PROFILE=saml rosa list users -c alebedev-test
#   INFO: There are no users configured for cluster 'alebedev-test'
#
# You create a credential, then `oc login`, and oc writes your local
# ~/.kube/config. That is the only kubeconfig that exists.
#
# (The guest.kubeconfig in the repo root is from old HyperShift/flexy work.
#  Unrelated — do not reuse it.)

## Option taken: managed htpasswd cluster-admin (quick, throwaway clusters)

AWS_PROFILE=saml rosa create admin -c alebedev-test

## Output

INFO: Admin account has been added to cluster 'alebedev-test'.
INFO: Please securely store this generated password. If you lose this password you can delete and recreate the cluster admin user.
INFO: To login, run the following command:

   oc login https://api.alebedev-test.5199.s3.devshift.org:443 --username cluster-admin --password <REDACTED>

INFO: It may take several minutes for this access to become active.

## Login
#
# The 'several minutes' is real — the cluster-admin IdP has to roll out.
# Early attempts fail with 401 / "Login failed (401 Unauthorized)". Retry.

oc login https://api.alebedev-test.5199.s3.devshift.org:443 \
  --username cluster-admin --password <REDACTED>

# Console (same credentials):
#   https://console-openshift-console.apps.rosa.alebedev-test.5199.s3.devshift.org

## Alternative not taken: a real IdP
#
# For a cluster that sticks around, prefer an IdP over a generated admin:
#   AWS_PROFILE=saml rosa create idp -c alebedev-test --type github --mapping-method claim

## Cleanup

AWS_PROFILE=saml rosa delete admin -c alebedev-test
